.TH WB\-CHECK 1 "" "watchbill"
.SH NAME
wb\-check \- say whether the repository is at its revision
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwb\fR check [\-q | \-\-quiet]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Say whether the repository still is at the revision it is pinned to: each file under .watchbill/revision/ against the manifest, the manifest against the revision line of watchbill.yml, and that revision against the installed wb, and the project text for a placeholder the revision does not know. Ends with 0 when all of it holds, 1 when something differs. Writes nothing. A limit of session_limits in watchbill.yml higher than the rule table's, one that is not a whole number of at least 1, or a role or a key it does not know, is a difference too. So is each line of .watchbill/signers in the working tree that does not hold its form (<identity> roles="<role>,..." <key type> <key>), among them a role that is not accept, start, handover or answer, named by its line. So is a .watchbill/signers that is not in the working tree, or one with no key line: without a list on the default branch nothing that needs a signature counts, and wb prompt and wb start, which run this check, hand out nothing.
.PP
Where all of it holds, .watchbill/project/style.md is neither in the working tree nor in HEAD, and no runner of the organisation's file is marked local: true, it says on standard error, as wb init does, that the code style cannot be surveyed by a model on this machine, and what to put there. It still ends with 0.
.PP
\-q (\-\-quiet) leaves out the line that says all holds, and no difference.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Leave out the lines that only confirm what was done or say there was nothing to do; never a refusal, a warning, what is said before a write, or a command the person has to run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All of it holds.
.TP
.B 1
Something differs.
.TP
.B 2
It was used wrongly or the repository is not set up.
.SH SEE ALSO
